+.. _hookimpl_arg_default:
+
+Hookimpl argument defaults
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+A *hookimpl* may declare a default value for an argument. The value the
+*hookimpl* receives for such an argument is determined in this order:
+
+1. the value passed by the hook call,
+2. the default value declared by the *hookspec*,
+3. the default value declared by the *hookimpl* itself.
+
+The *hookimpl*'s own default is therefore only used when neither the caller
+nor the *hookspec* provides the argument, for example when a new *hookimpl*
+runs against an older *hookspec* which does not have the argument yet:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    @hookspec
+    def myhook(config, args):
+        pass
+
+
+    @hookimpl
+    def myhook(config, args, new_arg="fallback"):
+        # new_arg is "fallback" with the old hookspec, and the passed
+        # value (or the hookspec default) once the hookspec gains new_arg.
+        print(new_arg)
+
+This applies to plain *hookimpls* as well as to wrappers and old-style
+hook wrappers.
+
+.. versionchanged:: 1.7
+    Previously the *hookimpl*'s default was always used, even when the hook
+    call passed an explicit value for the argument.
 
 
